WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:24.155
<v Host>Welcome to Japanese Daily News. I'm your host. Today is May twenty-fifth. In today's episode, we have one story: the man who created Japan's convenience store culture has passed away at ninety-three. It's a big deal — convenience stores are everywhere in Japan, and it all started with one person. Let's listen.

00:00:24.756 --> 00:00:40.884
<v News>セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。九十三歳でした。死因は心不全です。

00:00:41.584 --> 00:00:56.008
<v News>鈴木さんは、千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。セブンイレブンです。

00:00:56.707 --> 00:01:08.108
<v News>それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。鈴木さんがアメリカのセブンイレブンを日本に持ってきたのです。

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:24.504
<v News>今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。セブンイレブン、ローソン、ファミリーマート。日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:45.748
<v News>鈴木さんは「お客様の立場で考えなさい」といつも言っていました。おにぎり、お弁当、ATM、宅配便の受け取り。コンビニのサービスは、鈴木さんのこの考え方から生まれました。

00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:58.304
<v News>二千十六年に会長を退任し、名誉顧問になりました。日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

00:01:59.004 --> 00:02:02.555
<v Host>All right. Let's go through this piece by piece.

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:04.932
<v Host>The first sentence:

00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:17.867
<v Slow>セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。

00:02:18.668 --> 00:02:27.740
<v Host>Suzuki Toshifumi, honorary advisor of Seven and I Holdings, passed away on May eighteenth. Let's look at the key vocabulary.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.147
<v Vocab>亡くなりました

00:02:31.548 --> 00:02:39.587
<v Host>Passed away. This is the polite, respectful way to say someone died in Japanese. You'll hear this in news all the time.

00:02:40.187 --> 00:02:42.756
<v Vocab>死にました

00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:48.915
<v Host>The casual way sounds too direct — almost rude when talking about a real person.

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:52.180
<v Vocab>名誉顧問

00:02:52.580 --> 00:03:05.108
<v Host>Honorary advisor. This is a title given to retired executives. They no longer run the company, but they keep an advisory role. You'll see this title a lot in Japanese corporate news.

00:03:05.707 --> 00:03:06.788
<v Host>Next:

00:03:07.388 --> 00:03:19.891
<v Slow>千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。

00:03:20.692 --> 00:03:36.364
<v Host>In nineteen seventy-four, he opened the first convenience store in Japan, in Toyosu, Tokyo. That's over fifty years ago. Toyosu, by the way, is now famous for its fish market — the one that replaced Tsukiji.

00:03:36.963 --> 00:03:39.963
<v Vocab>第一号店

00:03:40.364 --> 00:03:44.948
<v Host>Store number one — the very first store. 'Gou' means 'number.'

00:03:45.548 --> 00:03:47.395
<v Vocab>店

00:03:47.795 --> 00:03:52.259
<v Host>And this means 'store.' You'll see this pattern for other firsts too.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.948
<v Vocab>出す

00:03:55.348 --> 00:04:05.788
<v Host>It literally means 'to put out,' but here it means to open a store. Japanese has many verbs like this where the meaning shifts depending on context. For example:

00:04:06.388 --> 00:04:09.460
<v Slow>手紙を出す。

00:04:10.259 --> 00:04:14.147
<v Host>To send a letter. Same verb, different meaning.

00:04:14.747 --> 00:04:17.459
<v Host>Then we hear why this was such a big deal:

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:24.300
<v Slow>それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:37.100
<v Host>Until then, there were no convenience stores in Japan. Think about that — no Seven Eleven, no Lawson, no Family Mart. None. He brought the whole concept from America.

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:40.052
<v Vocab>それまで

00:04:40.451 --> 00:04:50.004
<v Host>Until then. A very useful time expression. It points back to a time you already mentioned. You can use it in everyday conversation too:

00:04:50.603 --> 00:04:54.660
<v Slow>それまで知りませんでした。

00:04:55.459 --> 00:04:57.283
<v Host>I didn't know until then.

00:04:57.884 --> 00:05:01.627
<v Host>The news talks about how huge convenience stores are today:

00:05:02.228 --> 00:05:09.692
<v Slow>今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。

00:05:10.492 --> 00:05:18.675
<v Host>Today, there are approximately fifty-six thousand convenience stores in Japan. That's one for roughly every two thousand people.

00:05:19.276 --> 00:05:21.315
<v Vocab>約

00:05:21.716 --> 00:05:29.420
<v Host>Approximately. Short, common, and very useful. You'll hear it constantly in news whenever numbers come up.

00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:34.771
<v Host>And then a powerful sentence about what convenience stores mean to Japan:

00:05:35.372 --> 00:05:41.468
<v Slow>日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:45.627
<v Host>They became something indispensable to life in Japan.

00:05:46.228 --> 00:05:48.747
<v Vocab>欠かせない

00:05:49.148 --> 00:06:03.211
<v Host>Indispensable, can't do without. This is a strong word. If you've been to Japan, you know it's true — convenience stores are where you pay bills, pick up packages, buy concert tickets, and of course, eat.

00:06:03.812 --> 00:06:06.836
<v Host>Now, Suzuki's famous philosophy:

00:06:07.435 --> 00:06:12.355
<v Slow>お客様の立場で考えなさい。

00:06:13.156 --> 00:06:20.139
<v Host>Think from the customer's perspective. This one sentence explains why Japanese convenience stores are so good.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.947
<v Vocab>立場

00:06:23.348 --> 00:06:30.427
<v Host>Standpoint, perspective. A very common word in Japanese. You can use it with different particles:

00:06:31.028 --> 00:06:35.180
<v Slow>私の立場から言うと。

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:40.324
<v Host>Speaking from my standpoint. Very useful in discussions.

00:06:40.923 --> 00:06:44.307
<v Host>The grammar point here is the polite command form.

00:06:44.908 --> 00:06:47.091
<v Vocab>なさい

00:06:47.492 --> 00:06:52.028
<v Host>— a polite command form. It's softer than the blunt imperative.

00:06:52.627 --> 00:06:55.052
<v Vocab>考えろ

00:06:55.451 --> 00:07:02.603
<v Host>but it's still top-down. A boss quoting this to employees, a parent to a child — that's the tone.

00:07:03.204 --> 00:07:04.908
<v Host>And the final line:

00:07:05.507 --> 00:07:11.723
<v Slow>日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

00:07:12.524 --> 00:07:16.291
<v Host>He is called the father of Japan's convenience store industry.

00:07:16.891 --> 00:07:19.267
<v Vocab>産業

00:07:19.668 --> 00:07:25.596
<v Host>Industry. A formal word, mostly used in news and business contexts.

00:07:26.196 --> 00:07:29.052
<v Vocab>呼ばれています

00:07:29.451 --> 00:07:34.036
<v Host>Is being called. This is the passive form of the verb to call.

00:07:34.636 --> 00:07:36.651
<v Vocab>呼ぶ

00:07:37.052 --> 00:07:49.651
<v Host>To call. In the passive form, it becomes 'yobareru,' meaning 'to be called.' Add the ongoing state, and you get 'is being called' — a very natural way to say 'is known as' in Japanese.

00:07:50.252 --> 00:07:52.915
<v Vocab>呼ばれている

00:07:53.315 --> 00:07:59.411
<v Host>This is the ongoing state form. It's a very natural way to say 'is known as' in Japanese.

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:05.603
<v Host>Now let's listen to the full story one more time. See how much more you catch.

00:08:06.204 --> 00:08:22.331
<v News>セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。九十三歳でした。死因は心不全です。

00:08:23.031 --> 00:08:37.456
<v News>鈴木さんは、千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。セブンイレブンです。

00:08:38.155 --> 00:08:49.556
<v News>それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。鈴木さんがアメリカのセブンイレブンを日本に持ってきたのです。

00:08:50.255 --> 00:09:05.951
<v News>今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。セブンイレブン、ローソン、ファミリーマート。日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

00:09:06.652 --> 00:09:27.196
<v News>鈴木さんは「お客様の立場で考えなさい」といつも言っていました。おにぎり、お弁当、ATM、宅配便の受け取り。コンビニのサービスは、鈴木さんのこの考え方から生まれました。

00:09:27.895 --> 00:09:39.751
<v News>二千十六年に会長を退任し、名誉顧問になりました。日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

00:09:40.451 --> 00:09:45.635
<v Host>Quick quiz. Three questions in Japanese — try to answer before I do.

00:09:46.235 --> 00:09:53.748
<v Slow>鈴木さんは何年にコンビニの第一号店を出しましたか？

00:09:54.548 --> 00:10:02.539
<v Host>What year did Suzuki open the first convenience store? The answer: nineteen seventy-four. In Toyosu, Tokyo.

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:09.211
<v Slow>今、日本にコンビニは何店ありますか？

00:10:10.011 --> 00:10:15.772
<v Host>How many convenience stores are there in Japan today? Approximately fifty-six thousand.

00:10:16.371 --> 00:10:21.868
<v Slow>鈴木さんはいつも何と言っていましたか？

00:10:22.668 --> 00:10:40.716
<v Host>What did Suzuki always say? Think from the customer's perspective. Vocabulary recap. Passed away. Honorary advisor. The very first store. Until then. Approximately. Indispensable. Standpoint, perspective. Industry.

00:10:41.316 --> 00:10:53.700
<v Host>That's today's Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. Subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

00:10:54.299 --> 00:10:56.556
<v News>また明日。
